WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:07.432
Meet Andaith. A personal scheduler that connects planned blocks, reminders, and check-ins.

00:00:08.032 --> 00:00:15.208
It runs on a home server and sends reminders through Telegram. I built it for my own use.

00:00:16.808 --> 00:00:22.933
I wanted a clearer connection between what I planned and what I actually recorded.

00:00:23.533 --> 00:00:33.333
But asking for more input was not always helpful. Some moments need a check-in. Others only need a reminder.

00:00:34.933 --> 00:00:40.558
Let’s follow Alex, a fictional person working on a project called Atlas.

00:00:41.158 --> 00:00:49.733
First, Alex plans a block to prepare an example. That becomes a scheduled block with a start time and duration.

00:00:51.333 --> 00:01:00.083
When the block begins, Andaith sends a reminder. Alex replies with an intention: prepare a minimal example.

00:01:00.683 --> 00:01:08.783
At the end, Alex records the result. The original plan and the reported activity remain separate.

00:01:10.383 --> 00:01:16.808
If Alex does not respond to another block, Andaith records that no check-in was received.

00:01:17.408 --> 00:01:24.083
That does not mean the activity never happened. It means there is no response recorded.

00:01:25.683 --> 00:01:32.258
External agent routines can also prepare contextual digests and send them through Andaith.

00:01:32.858 --> 00:01:41.983
Here, a fictional event illustrates that connection. The agent gathers information; Andaith handles the notification.

00:01:43.583 --> 00:01:50.108
I’m exploring integrations that make sense for this project, while continuing to learn from using it.

00:01:50.708 --> 00:01:58.898
Interested in trying Andaith? Let’s talk. Have a similar challenge or an idea? I’d love to hear about it.
